BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Blood and Fame Book Launch
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260818T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260818T190000
UID:bm-5808420260818@shop.benmcnallybooks.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/7600763/Website2/
 Events/2026_08_18.png?cb=1783965646
DESCRIPTION:[at Ben McNally Books]\nJoin us at Ben McNally Books for an ev
 ening of movies and murder. Peter Rowe will be launching\nBlood and Fame\n
 (Crime Ink Press)\, and he'll be joined by Christopher Heard\, the author 
 of\nWilliam Friedkin\n(Sutherland Press).\nAbout the Books:\nBlood and Fam
 e\nA riveting new look at the parallel lives of Charles Manson and Roman P
 olanski reveals connections beyond the notorious Tate-LaBianca murders.\nS
 ince the horrific murder of Sharon Tate in 1969\, the names Charles Manson
  and Roman Polanski have been brutally linked. But new analysis of the par
 allel and intertwining stories of these two men reveals a connection that 
 preceded that fateful day.\nManson and Polanski shared remarkably similar\
 , and similarly awful\, childhoods. In their early years they both develop
 ed a hatred of the establishment and of authority—Manson\, from being ab
 used by relatives\, school staff\, and jailors\, and Polanski from living 
 under the Nazi regime and then Polish Communists in the 1950s.\nManson was
  released from prison and promptly went to the wildest place in North Amer
 ica (San Francisco’s Haight Ashbury) at around the same time as Polanski
  got out of communist Poland and headed for the wildest place in Europe (s
 winging London). Both men went overboard with the new freedom of the era. 
 Their paths crossed in Hollywood in the late 1960s\, where Polanski had wi
 ld success\, Manson utter failure. Then on August 8\, 1969\, Manson orches
 trated the murder of Polanski’s wife\, actress Sharon Tate\, and her unb
 orn son.\nCombining international celebrity biography\, analysis of a high
 -profile murder\, and in-depth consideration of the lasting legacy of the 
 crime in Polanski’s directorial work\, Blood and Fame is a riveting inve
 stigation that will appeal to both film lovers and true crime readers alik
 e.\nOrder a copy of\nBlood and Fame\nhere\nWilliam Friedkin\nThe first bio
 graphy of acclaimed American film director known as “Hurricane Billy.”
 \nWilliam Friedkin—"Hurricane Billy"—was a fearless\, trailblazing fil
 mmaker of the 1970s American New Wave. Born a streetwise kid in Chicago\, 
 he burst onto the scene with The People vs. Paul Crump\, a documentary tha
 t helped commute a death row inmate’s sentence. His eclectic early work 
 included a film with Sonny and Cher and an adaptation of Harold Pinter’s
  first play.\nFriedkin soared to fame with The French Connection\, winning
  an Oscar for Best Director\, then shattered box-office records with The E
 xorcist. He risked everything for Sorcerer\, his audacious masterpiece\, a
 nd was vilified for Cruising. Reinventing himself yet again\, he crafted t
 he slick neo-noir To Live and Die in L.A. in the 1980s.\nLoved\, loathed\,
  admired\, and imitated\, Friedkin was a cinematic risk-taker and master s
 toryteller\, known for his contradictions\, sharp wit\, and relentless pur
 suit of visceral\, uncompromising cinema. His legacy remains as bold and u
 npredictable as his films.\nOrder a copy of\nWilliam Friedkin\nhere
LOCATION:Ben McNally Books
URL:https://shop.benmcnallybooks.com/events
X-SOURCE:Ben McNally Books
END:VEVENT
